exp 6:
Step 1: Verify Docker installation

Check the installed Docker version.

docker --version

Expected output (your system):

Docker version 29.1.3, build 29.1.3-0ubuntu4.1
Step 2: Check Docker service status
sudo service docker status

If Docker is not active, start it:

sudo service docker start

Verify that Docker can communicate with the engine:

docker ps

This displays the currently running containers. An empty table is normal if no containers are running.

Step 3: Run the Hello World container

Execute:

sudo docker run hello-world

Docker downloads the hello-world image if it is not already available, creates a container, and displays a confirmation message.

Expected output:

Hello from Docker!
This message shows that your installation appears to be working correctly.
Step 4: List Docker images
docker images

This displays the images stored locally, including their repository names, tags, IDs, and sizes.

Images in your output included hello-world, nginx, and a Minikube image.

Step 5: Download and run a BusyBox container

Run the following command on one line:

docker run -d --name demo busybox sh -c "while true; do echo hello; sleep 5; done"

Explanation:

docker run creates and starts a container.

-d runs it in the background.

--name demo assigns the container the name demo.

busybox specifies the image.

sh -c executes the supplied shell command.

while true repeats continuously, printing hello every five seconds.

Docker automatically downloads BusyBox if the image is not available locally.

Important: Run this command only once while the container named demo exists. If you already created it, continue with Step 6 rather than running it again.

Step 6: Verify the running container
docker ps

Expected output will contain a row similar to:

CONTAINER ID   IMAGE     STATUS          NAMES
993a9fe75ca3   busybox   Up ...          demo

The container ID and elapsed time may differ. The Up status indicates that the container is running.

To view its printed messages:

docker logs demo
Step 7: Create a file on the host system

Create a text file in your current working directory:

echo "Welcome to Docker" > message.txt

Verify its contents:

cat message.txt

Expected output:

Welcome to Docker
Step 8: Copy the file into the container

Use the correct docker cp syntax:

docker cp message.txt demo:/tmp/message.txt

Here:

message.txt is the source file on Ubuntu.

demo is the container name.

/tmp/message.txt is the destination inside the container.

Remember: Do not put a space between demo and :/tmp/message.txt.

Verify the copied file:

docker exec demo cat /tmp/message.txt

Expected output:

Welcome to Docker

docker exec runs a command inside an already running container.

Step 9: Inspect the container

Execute:

docker container inspect demo

This displays detailed information about the container in JSON format, including:

Container ID and name

Image used

Running or stopped status

Startup command

Network configuration and IP address

Mounts and other configuration details

To display only its current status, you can use:

docker inspect -f '{{.State.Status}}' demo

Expected output before stopping:

running
Step 10: Export the container filesystem

Run:

docker container export demo > demo-backup.tar

This exports the container's filesystem as a TAR archive in the current directory.

Check that the archive exists:

ls -lh demo-backup.tar

Note: docker export exports the container filesystem, but it does not preserve the image history or container configuration. It is not a complete backup of all Docker metadata or mounted volumes.

Step 11: Stop the container

Stop the running demo container:

docker stop demo

Expected output:

demo

Check the running containers:

docker ps

The demo container should no longer appear because it has stopped.

To view both running and stopped containers, execute:

docker ps -a

You should see demo with an Exited status.

Step 12: Verify the exported archive

List the files in your current directory:

ls

You should find these files:

message.txt
demo-backup.tar

This confirms that the host file was created and the container filesystem was exported.
